<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>4.15.2: Additional Resources</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m01135</md:content-id>
    <md:title>4.15.2: Additional Resources</md:title>
    <md:uuid>e258c50c-8e3f-4944-a051-0cd37e0a396f</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
  <section><title>Graphs of Sequences</title>
<para>Graph the sequence {18, 36, 72, 144, 288, . . .}.</para>
<para>First, make a table:</para>
<table class="os-raise-horizontaltable">
  <thead/>
  <tbody>
    <row>
      <entry><emphasis>Term</emphasis></entry><entry>
        1
      </entry>
      <entry>
        2
      </entry>
      <entry>
        3
      </entry>
      <entry>
        4
      </entry>
      <entry>
        5
      </entry>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry><emphasis>Value</emphasis></entry><entry>
        18
      </entry>
      <entry>
        36
      </entry>
      <entry>
        72
      </entry>
      <entry>
        144
      </entry>
      <entry>
        288
      </entry>
    </row>
  </tbody>
</table>

<para>Now, plot the points:</para>
<para><figure><media alt="Graph of the geometric sequence."><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/f80cefc97907cdc250dfe7949abf7d406fdc14ed"/></media></figure></para>
<para>Notice that each height is double the previous height.</para>
<para>Each term is also double the previous term.</para>
<title>Try It: Graphs of Sequences</title>
<para>Graph the following sequence and explain the pattern.</para>

<example><exercise>
  <problem>
    
    <para>1, 3, 9, 27, . . .</para>
  <para>Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.
    </para>
  </problem><solution>
  <para>Here is how to determine the pattern of a sequence with a graph:</para>
  <para>First, make a table:</para>
  <table class="os-raise-horizontaltable">
    <thead/>
    <tbody>
      <row>
        <entry><emphasis>Term</emphasis></entry><entry>
          1
        </entry>
        <entry>
          2
        </entry>
        <entry>
          3
        </entry>
        <entry>
          4
        </entry>
      </row>
      <row>
        <entry><emphasis>Value</emphasis></entry><entry>
          1
        </entry>
        <entry>
          3
        </entry>
        <entry>
          9
        </entry>
        <entry>
          127
        </entry>
      </row>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  
  <para>Next, plot the points:</para>
  <para><figure><media alt="GRAPH SHOWING PLOTTED POINTS (1, 1), (2, 3), (3, 9), AND (4, 27)."><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/9e69225cf486825f1b31d8331149e987257b2600"/></media></figure></para>
  <para>Each point is 3 times the height of the previous point.</para>
  <para>Notice that in the sequence, each term is 3 times the previous term.</para>
</solution></exercise>
</example></section>
</content>
</document>
